Gantt chart implementation
==========================

Redmine::Gantt handles request parameters and user preferences. It is
also the entry point for HTML, PDF and PNG. The implementation lives under
lib/redmine/gantt; the chart entry partial is app/views/gantts/_chart.html.erb,
with component partials under app/views/gantts/chart. Navigation parameters belong
in GanttHelper, presentation
attributes and icons in Gantts::ChartHelper, and browser interaction in the
Stimulus controllers under app/javascript/controllers/gantt.

Data and display sections
-------------------------

Dataset owns the IssueQuery result, visible project ancestors, versions,
relations, ordering and global row limit. each_project yields a project, its
depth and its available row budget. each_row yields the record, depth, display
key and parent key. HTML and both exports use this traversal.

ProjectSection represents one project's own rows, including its versions and
issues. Subprojects are separate sections. Shared versions have a different
display key in each project, while issue keys remain stable.

  gantt.project_section(project)  # nil when outside the displayed dataset
  section.each_row { |row| ... }  # build and consume each HTML ViewModel
  section.row_count              # bounded count without constructing ViewModels

The lookup preserves the full query and global row budget. It does not build
the other projects' ViewModels, but still loads the query's bounded issue set
to establish global membership. It is not a separately filtered project query.

Chart contains layout, scale, relations and sections. Sections capture the
request context, then build frozen typed rows during rendering. They do not
retain every derived row until the response is complete. The rows convenience
methods materialize arrays; rendering should use each_row instead.

The source records and their associations are ordinary Active Record objects.
These objects are request-local, not deeply immutable or suitable for caching
between requests or users. Pass query and project as initialization keywords. To change the search
context, construct a new Gantt instead of mutating its query or project.

Each section has its own HTML wrapper (gantt-project-ID). A future partial
update must also refresh affected ancestor aggregates, shared versions, and
cross-project relation metadata. A change to membership or the global row limit
may affect other sections. No Turbo endpoint or mutation API is introduced here.

Outputs
-------

Schedule calculates date offsets without rounding fractional days. Each output
converts them to pixels or PDF units and rounds there. PDF and PNG use the same
date calculation without constructing HTML rows.

Exports::PDF retains ITCPDF pagination and drawing. Exports::Image retains
MiniMagick drawing and configuration. Exports::Base supplies the common row
traversal and labels. PNG remains available only when MiniMagick is loaded.
Browser printing uses the HTML chart and is distinct from PDF export.
Before printing, visible rows are positioned on a continuous vertical plane so
page fragmentation cannot move them independently of the SVG relation overlay.
After printing, the normal flow layout is restored.
The document takes the chart's intrinsic width when printing so the browser can
shrink it as it did the old table. The chart keeps its initial row count for
minimum height, including when rows are collapsed.

Verification
------------

Run the Gantt unit, helper, controller and system tests. For visual changes,
compare both screen and browser-print output against the original chart with
identical data, dates, zoom, columns, relation/progress options and print settings.
For export changes, compare rendered PDF pages and PNG pixels, including multiple
pages and shared versions. For memory changes, use identical database snapshots
and fresh server processes; compare response time, allocations, full-GC RSS and
peak RSS. Allocation counts alone do not establish a memory improvement.

A Japanese component guide is available in doc/GANTT_ARCHITECTURE.md.
